(function () {
  function P(q, a, h, s) { return { q: q, a: a, h: h || "", s: s || "" }; }
  function E(exam, q, a, h, s) { var x = P(q, a, h, s); x.exam = exam; return x; }

  var SSC_JE_MECHENG = {};

  SSC_JE_MECHENG.mechatronics = [
    P("What is mechatronics?", "Mechatronics is the synergistic integration of mechanical engineering, electronics, computer science and control engineering to design intelligent products and systems.", "Combination of mechanics + electronics + computing", "A mechatronic system involves sensors (measure), actuators (act), a controller (decide) and mechanical structure. Examples: CNC machine, ABS, robot, autofocus camera."),
    P("Distinguish between a sensor and a transducer.", "A sensor detects a physical quantity and converts it into a measurable signal. A transducer converts one form of energy into another form. Every sensor contains a transducer but not every transducer is a sensor.", "Sensor measures; transducer converts", "Thermocouple (temperature \u2192 voltage) is both a sensor and a transducer. A potentiometer is a transducer whose resistance changes with displacement."),
    P("Explain the working principle of an LVDT.", "LVDT (Linear Variable Differential Transformer) works on mutual inductance change. A movable ferromagnetic core couples the primary winding with two secondary windings wound in opposition; displacement moves the core and produces a voltage proportional to displacement.", "Differential transformer with moving core", "At the null position the induced voltages in the two secondaries cancel to zero. When the core moves, one secondary voltage increases and the other decreases; the difference gives magnitude and the phase gives direction. Used to measure linear displacement (range \u00B1 mm to \u00B1 cm)."),
    P("Define gauge factor of a strain gauge.", "Gauge factor GF = (\u0394R/R) / (\u0394L/L) = (\u0394R/R) / \u03B5, where R = resistance, L = length, \u03B5 = strain. For metallic foil gauges GF \u2248 2.", "Ratio of fractional resistance change to strain", "GF depends on the piezoresistive effect and Poisson's ratio. Semiconductor strain gauges can have GF 50\u2013200. Strain gauge needs temperature compensation (self-compensating gauge or dummy gauge in a Wheatstone bridge)."),
    P("State the three laws of thermocouples.", "1. Law of homogeneous materials: an EMF is generated only in a homogeneous circuit when temperature differs. 2. Law of intermediate materials: if a third metal is inserted, total EMF is unchanged provided its two junctions are at the same temperature. 3. Law of intermediate temperatures: EMF at T1,T3 equals sum of EMFs at T1,T2 and T2,T3.", "Homogeneous, intermediate material, intermediate temperature", "These laws let us insert a voltmeter and calibrate junctions in practical temperature measurement. Common pairs: K (chromel\u2013alumel), J (iron\u2013constantan), R/S (Pt\u2013Rh)."),
    P("What is a stepper motor? Name its types.", "A stepper motor rotates in discrete fixed angular steps for each input pulse. Types: variable reluctance (VR), permanent magnet (PM) and hybrid stepper motors.", "Motor that steps per pulse", "Step angle = 360 / (no. of teeth \u00D7 phases). For a 1.8\u00B0 motor, 200 steps/revolution. Steppers are used in open-loop position control (printers, CNC, robotics) because no feedback encoder is needed."),
    P("Difference between incremental and absolute encoders.", "An incremental encoder gives pulses proportional to rotation (relative position, needs a home/reference point). An absolute encoder gives a unique digital code for every angular position, preserving position after power loss.", "Relative vs absolute position", "Incremental: quadrature outputs A and B allow direction sensing, plus Z index pulse per revolution. Absolute: has N tracks of discs giving 2^N positions; used where position must survive power-off (machine tool axes)."),
    P("Compare PLC, microcontroller and microprocessor.", "PLC: rugged industrial controller with relays/ladder logic, IO modules, real-time scan cycle, used in factories. Microcontroller: single-chip computer (CPU+RAM+ROM+IO) for embedded control (e.g., 8051, ARM). Microprocessor: only CPU, needs external memory/peripherals (PC CPUs).", "Industrial controller vs chip vs CPU", "PLC is programmable, replaces hard-wired relay panels. Microcontrollers are cheap and used in consumer/embedded electronics. PLCs withstand noise, vibration and high temperature."),
    P("What is PWM and where is it used?", "PWM (Pulse Width Modulation) controls average power by varying the duty cycle of a fixed-frequency square wave: V_avg = V_supply \u00D7 duty cycle. Used in speed control of DC motors, dimming LEDs, and DAC.", "Varying duty cycle to control power", "Duty cycle = Ton/(Ton+Toff) \u00D7 100%. A 50% duty at 12 V gives an average 6 V, but peak remains 12 V, so a motor sees a smoother effective voltage due to its inductance."),
    P("List the types of robots by coordinate geometry.", "1. Cartesian (PPP) - three linear joints, rigid precision. 2. Cylindrical (RPP) - base rotation + vertical/radial slides. 3. Spherical/Polar (RRP) - rotation + elevation + radial. 4. Articulated/Revolute (RRR) - like human arm. 5. SCARA (RRP horizontal) - rigid vertical, compliant horizontal. 6. Delta (parallel) - high speed picking.", "How the robot arm moves through space", "Most industrial robots (6-axis) are articulated, giving a spherical workspace. SCARA is fastest for vertical insertions (electronics assembly). Delta systems excel at pick-and-place of light parts."),
    P("Define the degrees of freedom (DOF) of a robot.", "DOF is the number of independent joint motions (translations/rotations) needed to completely specify the position and orientation of the end-effector in 3D space. A free rigid body has 6 DOF (3 translational + 3 rotational).", "Independent axes of motion", "A typical 6-axis articulated robot can place the end-effector at any position and orientation \u2014 known as a 6-DOF robot. Gripper opening/closing is not counted as a DOF."),
    P("Explain the terms workspace and payload of a robot.", "Workspace is the set of all points reachable by the end-effector; payload is the maximum mass the robot can carry at full speed (often specified at a defined point and velocity).", "Reachable volume and load capacity", "Workspace depends on link lengths and joint limits; it shrinks if we require a specific orientation. Payload reduces when the robot moves at high speed or with a long reach."),
    P("What do you understand by forward and inverse kinematics?", "Forward kinematics: given joint variables (angles, displacements), find the position/orientation of the end-effector (unique). Inverse kinematics: given a desired end-effector pose, find the joint variables (may have 0, 1 or many solutions).", "Joints \u2192 pose vs pose \u2192 joints", "Forward kinematics uses the geometric transformation matrices (DH parameters) multiplied along the chain. Inverse kinematics is solved by closed-form or iterative numerical methods and is harder."),
    P("Classify robot grippers.", "Grippers: 1. Mechanical (pneumatic/hydraulic/servo driven fingers, 2 or 3 fingers). 2. Vacuum (suction cups) for flat/porous parts. 3. Magnetic (electromagnet/permanent) for ferrous parts. 4. Adhesive/soft grippers. 5. Special purpose (tool changers, hooks).", "How the robot holds the work", "Choice depends on part shape, weight, surface finish, and need for force control. Two-finger parallel grippers are the most common for pick-and-place."),
    P("Compare hydraulic, pneumatic and electric actuators in robotics.", "Hydraulic: high force/power density, stiff, needs pump+oil, expensive mess. Pneumatic: low cost, clean, fast, limited force/accuracy (stiction). Electric (servo/stepper): precise, easy control, clean, moderate force, most common in robotics.", "Force vs speed vs precision", "Hydraulics suit heavy robotic applications (construction, forging). Pneumatics suit simple pick-and-place. Electric servos dominate industrial robots for precision and controllability."),
    P("What is a Hall-effect sensor used for?", "A Hall-effect sensor produces a voltage transverse to current flow when placed in a magnetic field; it measures magnetic field, proximity, position, speed, and current.", "Detects magnetic field via Hall voltage", "Applications: brushless DC motor commutation, wheel speed sensors (ABS), current sensors, rotary encoders, and keyboard switch detection (magnetic reedless)."),
    P("What is the working principle of an inductive proximity sensor?", "An inductive proximity sensor uses a high-frequency oscillating electromagnetic field; a metallic target induces eddy currents that damp the oscillation, toggling the output. It detects only conductive metals.", "Eddy-current damping of oscillator", "Range is small (1\u201340 mm). Capacitive sensors detect both metal and non-metal (liquid, plastic) by change in capacitance. Photoelectric sensors use light reflection/beam interrupt and have long ranges."),
    P("Explain P, PI and PID controllers.", "P: output proportional to error (Kp\u00B7e) - reduces steady error but can oscillate. PI: adds integral action (Ki\u222B e dt) - removes steady-state offset, may slow response. PID: adds derivative action (Kd\u00B7de/dt) - predicts error, damps oscillations, improves stability.", "Proportional + Integral + Derivative action", "PID tuning methods: Ziegler\u2013Nichols (ultimate gain or reaction curve). Too much D adds noise sensitivity. In process control, most loops are PI or PID."),
    P("What is a PLC ladder diagram?", "A ladder diagram is a graphical programming language for PLCs that mimics hard-wired relay logic: rungs contain contacts (inputs) in series/parallel feeding coils (outputs).", "Relay-style PLC program", "Example rung: |--[START]--[STOP_NC]--(MOTOR)--|. Contacts read inputs, coils drive outputs; the PLC scans rungs repeatedly (scan cycle typically ms). Rungs can also call timers, counters and comparison blocks."),
    P("What is a tachometer used for in a mechatronic system?", "A tachometer (DC generator/encoder) measures rotational speed and provides velocity feedback to a servo loop so the controller can stabilize and precisely control motor speed.", "Speed sensor feedback", "DC tachogenerator gives voltage proportional to speed; optical encoders give pulses counted per second. Velocity feedback (damping term) is essential to prevent overshoot in servo systems."),
    P("What is the difference between open-loop and closed-loop control?", "Open-loop: output is not measured; control action depends only on the set command (no feedback). Closed-loop: output is measured by a sensor and compared with the reference; error drives correction (feedback control).", "Feedback present or not", "Open-loop is simpler and cheap but drifts under disturbances (washing machine timer). Closed-loop is accurate and robust (room AC thermostat, CNC servo, PID)."),
    P("State Asimov's Three Laws of Robotics.", "1. A robot may not injure a human being or allow one to come to harm through inaction. 2. A robot must obey human orders except where they conflict with the First Law. 3. A robot must protect its own existence unless this conflicts with the First or Second Law.", "Safety laws for robots", "These are a fictional/ethical framework by Isaac Asimov used to discuss robot safety and autonomy; modern collaborative robots follow similar safety logic (force/torque limited, speed limits, safety zones)."),
    P("What is the Jacobian in robotics?", "The Jacobian J maps joint velocities to end-effector velocities: v = J\u00B7\u03B8\u0307. Near singular configurations det(J) \u2192 0, and the robot loses DOF/control authority.", "Velocity mapping matrix", "The Jacobian is used to study velocity and force analysis (F = J^T\u00B7\u03C4), to find singularities, and in inverse kinematics and path control. Parallel robots have strong singularities inside their workspace."),
    P("What is meant by a collaborative robot (cobot)?", "A cobot is designed to work safely side-by-side with humans without heavy guarding, using low speed, limited force, torque sensors and vision, stopping instantly on contact.", "Human-safe robot", "Cobots (Universal Robots etc.) use lightweight design, rounded shapes, and sensitive joint torque sensors. They automate tasks humans find tiring/repetitive and are easy to program by hand-guiding."),
    P("What is a convolutional neural network doing in modern 'robot vision'?", "A CNN is a deep-learning architecture that processes images through convolution, pooling and fully-connected layers to recognize objects, poses or defects, giving robots vision for picking, sorting and inspection.", "Deep learning image recognition", "For pick-and-place, a CNN + camera localizes objects (pose estimation), then inverse kinematics drives the arm. Data-driven vision is robust to lighting, occlusion, and part variation.")
  ];

  SSC_JE_MECHENG.maintenance = [
    P("What are the objectives of maintenance engineering?", "Objectives: 1. Maximize equipment availability and reliability at minimum cost. 2. Prevent premature breakdowns and failures. 3. Extend useful life of machines. 4. Maintain product quality and safety. 5. Reduce downtime and energy losses.", "Keep machines running, safely and cheaply", "Maintenance is a profit activity \u2014 every hour of lost production is directly measurable. Modern aim is not zero failure but optimized life-cycle cost."),
    P("Classify the main types of maintenance.", "1. Corrective / Breakdown maintenance (repair after failure). 2. Preventive maintenance (scheduled servicing at fixed intervals). 3. Predictive / Condition-based maintenance (run-to-predict, act on monitored condition). 4. Total Productive Maintenance (TPM - company-wide). 5. Proactive/RCM based.", "Repair vs scheduled vs condition-based", "Breakdown: cheapest to set up, most expensive in practice. Preventive: fixed-time overhaul prevents random failures but may 'over-maintain'. Predictive: monitors vibration, temperature, oil, acoustic signatures and works only at need."),
    P("Define MTBF, MTTF and MTTR.", "MTBF = Mean Time Between Failures = total operating time / number of failures (repairable items). MTTF = Mean Time To Failure (non-repairable items). MTTR = Mean Time To Repair = total repair time / number of repairs.", "Uptime/downtime statistics", "MTBF and MTTR are measured from field data. High MTBF + low MTTR = high availability. These drive spares and man-power planning."),
    P("Write the formula for equipment availability.", "Availability A = MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR), or A = Uptime / (Uptime + Downtime).", "Fraction of time the unit is ready", "Example: MTBF = 90 h, MTTR = 10 h \u2192 A = 90/(90+10) = 0.9 (90%). Inherent availability uses only breakdown time; operational availability includes logistics and administrative delays."),
    P("How is reliability defined? Derive the series-parallel concept.", "Reliability R(t) = e^{-\u03BBt}, where \u03BB = constant failure rate. In series, system reliability = product of component reliabilities (unreliable). In parallel (redundancy), R = 1 \u2212 \u03A0(1 \u2212 Ri).", "Probability of survival to time t", "For two components in series with R = 0.9 each: Rs = 0.81. In parallel: Rp = 1 \u2212 0.1\u00D70.1 = 0.99. Redundancy (sparing) dramatically boosts reliability at extra cost."),
    P("What is failure rate \u03BB and the bathtub curve?", "Failure rate is failures per unit time. The bathtub curve has three zones: early 'infant mortality' (burn-in), constant random failures (useful life, \u03BB = constant), and wear-out (increasing \u03BB).", "Three phases of machine life", "Actions: burn-in/run-in reduces early failures; condition monitoring postpones wear-out; preventive/predictive maintenance keeps the constant-rate region long."),
    P("Explain FMEA in maintenance.", "FMEA (Failure Mode and Effects Analysis) is a systematic tabulated study that lists each failure mode, its cause, effect, and a Risk Priority Number (RPN = Severity \u00D7 Occurrence \u00D7 Detection) to prioritize corrective actions.", "Bottom-up failure study", "RPN \u2265 a threshold (e.g., 100) triggers action. FMECA adds 'C' = criticality. FMEA applied at design time is DFMEA; applied to processes is PFMEA."),
    P("What is RCM?", "RCM (Reliability Centered Maintenance) is a systematic process that determines the maintenance strategy for each asset based on the consequences of failure (safety, operational, environmental), asking FMEA-style questions per function.", "Maintenance selected by failure consequences", "RCM moves resources from time-based to condition-based tasks, and decides between preventive, predictive, redesign, or run-to-failure for each component."),
    P("Name the pillars of TPM.", "The 8 pillars of TPM: 1. Autonomous maintenance (operator). 2. Focused improvement (Kaizen). 3. Planned maintenance. 4. Quality maintenance. 5. Training & education. 6. Early equipment management. 7. Office TPM (administration). 8. Safety, health & environment.", "TPM 8-pillar structure", "Japanese TPM (Seiichi Nakajima) also requires the 5S foundation: Seiri, Seiton, Seiso, Seiketsu, Shitsuke (Sort, Set, Shine, Standardize, Sustain)."),
    P("How is OEE calculated?", "OEE = Availability \u00D7 Performance \u00D7 Quality. Availability = Run time / Planned time; Performance = (Actual output \u00D7 Ideal cycle time)/Run time; Quality = Good parts / Total parts.", "Overall Equipment Effectiveness", "World-class benchmark: OEE \u2265 85% (A=90%, P=95%, Q=99.9%). OEE exposes the six big losses: breakdowns, setup/adjustment, idling, reduced speed, defects, startup losses."),
    P("What is condition monitoring, and list its techniques.", "Condition monitoring uses measured signals to detect developing faults before breakdown. Techniques: vibration analysis, oil analysis (spectrometry, ferrography), thermography (infrared), acoustic/ultrasonic emission, motor current signature analysis, performance monitoring.", "Predict faults by watching signals", "Trending vibration levels reveals imbalance, misalignment, bearing wear, looseness. Condition monitoring demands reference baselines and saves money vs fixed internal overhaul."),
    P("What are the common causes of vibration in rotating machines?", "Imbalance (1\u00D7 RPM), misalignment (usually 2\u00D7 RPM), bent shaft, bearing faults (defect frequencies), gear faults, looseness, resonance, and flow-induced vibration.", "Diagnose vibration by frequency", "A unbalance peak appears at 1\u00D7 running speed; misalignment shows strong 2\u00D7. Bearing defect frequencies are computed from ball/race geometry and appear as high-frequency harmonics."),
    P("Classify lubrication regimes.", "1. Boundary lubrication - thin film, surfaces partially contact, high friction. 2. Mixed lubrication - partial partial. 3. Hydrodynamic lubrication - full fluid film separated by fluid pressure (bearing). 4. Elasto-hydrodynamic (EHD) - film deforms elastically (gears, rolling bearings).", "Fluid film thickness vs surface roughness", "Hydrodynamic bearings use wedge geometry and relative motion to build pressure (Z Sommerfeld). When film thickness \u226B surface roughness the regime is full-film / EHD."),
    P("Why is lubrication important in maintenance?", "Lubrication reduces friction and wear, removes heat, flushes particles, prevents rust and reduces noise; hence correct lubricant and change intervals extend machine life.", "The lifeblood of moving machinery", "About 50\u201360% of premature bearing failures are lubrication related (wrong grade, contamination, starvation). This makes condition-based oil analysis very cost-effective."),
    P("What is oil analysis and what does it detect?", "Oil analysis tests viscosity, TAN/TBN, water, particles (ISO cleanliness), and wear metals (Fe, Cu, Cr, Al) by spectroscopy/ferrography to detect contamination and internal wear trends.", "Blood test of the machine", "A sudden rise in iron suggests gear/bearing wear; silicon indicates dirt ingress; water or glycol indicates coolant leak. Oil sampling must follow a strict flush-sample procedure."),
    P("What is thermography in predictive maintenance?", "Infrared thermography maps surface temperature with a thermal camera to detect hot-spots: loose electrical connections, failing busbars, motor overheating, blocked coolers, and insulation failures.", "See heat before failure", "A 10\u00B0C rise above baseline on a motor bearing can multiply failure risk. Thermography requires emissivity settings and safe access; it detects faults that are invisible to vibration analysis."),
    P("What is preventive maintenance scheduling (PMS)?", "PMS plans fixed-interval tasks (daily, weekly, monthly, annual) such as lubrication, cleaning, inspection, adjustment and part replacement using checklists, work orders and schedules derived from OEM and equipment history.", "Fixed-period planned tasks", "Scheduling balances workload, uses PERT/CPM for major shutdowns, and records 'history cards' for every machine, enabling better planning and cost control."),
    P("What is the difference between corrective and preventive maintenance with examples?", "Corrective maintenance is reactive \u2014 repair or replace after the failure occurs (replace a burst seal). Preventive maintenance is proactive \u2014 scheduled activities before failure (replace seals every 6 months, oil change every 250 hours).", "After failure vs before failure", "Preventive maintenance is justified when failure cost (downtime + damage) exceeds maintenance cost. Random failures, however, cannot be prevented by time-based schedules \u2014 hence predictive maintenance."),
    P("What is run-to-failure maintenance, and when is it used?", "Run-to-failure deliberately lets a non-critical component fail and then replaces it. It is used when failure has negligible consequences and spare replacement is cheap and quick.", "Deliberately allow cheap failures", "Examples: light bulbs, small drive belts. It is part of RCM decision logic \u2014 if a failure has no safety impact and is cheap to repair, planned run-to-failure is optimal."),
    P("What is the purpose of a maintenance history record?", "The history card records each machine's installation, service, repairs, spares used, downtime and costs, enabling trend analysis, MTBF/MTTR computation, better planning and life-cycle cost decisions.", "Documented machine biography", "Without history records, preventive intervals are guesswork. Modern CMMS (Computerized Maintenance Management System) stores history, auto-generates work orders and tracks KPIs."),
    P("Distinguish between corrective maintenance and breakdown maintenance.", "Corrective maintenance can be planned (defect found in inspection, scheduled repair) or unplanned (breakdown). Breakdown maintenance is purely unplanned failure response. Corrective maintenance is wider, including defect rectification found during inspections.", "Planned vs unplanned repair", "A good program catches defects through inspection (condition monitoring) and corrects them in a planned window \u2014 converting unplanned breakdowns into planned corrective work."),
    P("What is a millwright / fitter's role in maintenance?", "A maintenance fitter (millwright) aligns, levels, fits and assembles machine components, removes/replaces mechanical parts, uses metrology tools, and conducts precision alignment (laser shaft alignment).", "Mechanical hands of maintenance", "Precision shaft alignment reduces vibration and doubles coupling/bearing life. Millwrights also manage tolerances, fits (interference/clearance/transition) and heat-fitting of bearings."),
    P("What is predictive vs preventive maintenance in one line each?", "Preventive maintenance works on a time schedule ('do it every month'), predictive maintenance works on measured condition ('do it when vibration exceeds a limit').", "Calendar vs condition trigger", "Predictive is generally smarter: it avoids over-maintenance and catches failure modes that time-based schedules miss, but needs investment in sensors and trained analysts."),
    P("What is a safety lockout-tagout (LOTO) procedure?", "LOTO isolates a machine from all energy sources (electrical, pneumatic, hydraulic, gravity, stored spring) with locks and tags before maintenance, so no one can re-energize it while a person is inside.", "Protect the maintenance person", "Steps: notify, shut down, isolate, apply locks/tags, release stored energy, verify zero energy, then work. It is a legal requirement (OSHA 1910.147) and prevents the classic 'someone started the machine' accidents."),
    P("What is the difference between MRO spares management and routine stores?", "MRO (Maintenance, Repair, Operations) stores hold critical spares (bearing, seals, motors) chosen by criticality; routine stores hold consumables. A, B, C (Pareto) classification sets stock levels and reorder policies (EOQ/ROP).", "Spares availability strategy", "Critical spares for failure modes with long lead time are stocked per machine, while slow-moving items may be shared or procured on demand. Under/over-stocking both cost money, so ABC + economic order quantity analysis is applied."),
    P("What is vibration analysis and how are severity limits set?", "Vibration analysis measures acceleration, velocity, displacement (per ISO 10816 for machines) and compares overall RMS velocity with severity zones (good, satisfactory, unsatisfactory, unacceptable) to schedule maintenance.", "Measure and compare with standards", "ISO 10816 classifies machines by power/support type; e.g., a large motor on rigid foundation is 'good' up to 2.8 mm/s RMS. Trend, not single reading, triggers action."),
    P("What are the six big losses addressed by TPM?", "1. Breakdown (equipment failure). 2. Setup/adjustment loss. 3. Idling/minor stoppages. 4. Reduced machine speed. 5. Quality defects / rework. 6. Startup/yield losses (scrap at start-up).", "The six loss categories", "OEE directly quantifies losses 1\u20134 (Availability/Performance) and 5\u20136 (Quality). Eliminating these through autonomous + planned maintenance is the goal of TPM."),
    P("What is baseline maintenance data used for?", "Baseline data (initial vibration, temperature, alignment, oil state) is recorded when equipment is new or overhauled; future readings are compared to this reference to detect deterioration early.", "The 'normal' reference fingerprint", "Without a baseline, a single reading cannot be interpreted. Baselines must be taken under the same load and speed conditions for valid comparisons."),
    P("Explain the principle of 'zero defects' and poka-yoke in reliability/maintenance.", "'Zero defects' (Shigeo Shingo) says defects are prevented by design changes and mistake-proofing (poka-yoke), not inspection. In maintenance, meaning error-proof equipment (color-coded connectors, keyed parts, torque limiters) prevents wrong assembly.", "Design out the possibility of error", "Poka-yoke devices are source-inspection mechanisms \u2014 e.g., a fixture that physically cannot be fitted wrongly, or run-to-failure sensors. This reduces human-error-driven failures, a top maintenance root cause.")
  ];

  SSC_JE_MECHENG.imor = [
    P("List the five functions of management (Henri Fayol).", "Planning, Organizing, Directing (Commanding), Coordinating, Controlling.", "Fayol's POCCC", "Later, Koontz & O'Donnell added Staffing, giving POSDCORB (Planning, Organizing, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, Budgeting after Gulick/Urwick). Management is an ongoing cycle of these functions."),
    P("What is the difference between management and administration?", "Administration sets objectives and policies (top-level, decision making), while management executes those policies and plans (middle/operational level).", "Policy making vs policy execution", "Administration is mainly a top-management function; management covers middle and lower levels. The two terms are often used interchangeably in modern practice."),
    P("State Fayol's key principles of management.", "Fourteen principles including: division of work, authority & responsibility, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, subordination of individual interest, remuneration, centralization, scalar chain, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, esprit de corps.", "Fayol's 14 principles", "Memorize the critical ones: one boss (unity of command), one plan (unity of direction), scalar chain (line of authority), discipline begins at the top. These are still the foundation of organizational design."),
    P("What is Taylor's Scientific Management?", "F. W. Taylor's method improves productivity through time and motion study, standardizing tools and work, differential piece-rate wages, functional foremanship, and a mental revolution (cooperation between labor and management).", "'One best way' + fair pay", "Taylor defined the 'one best way' by using Time Study + Motion Study. Differential piece rate pays a higher rate for exceeding standard output. Criticism: treats workers like machines."),
    P("Explain Maslow's hierarchy of needs.", "Maslow's hierarchy (in order): 1. Physiological, 2. Safety, 3. Social/Love, 4. Esteem, 5. Self-actualization. A satisfied need ceases to motivate.", "Five-level motivation pyramid", "For managers: money satisfies physiological+partially esteem; job security \u2192 safety; teamwork \u2192 social; recognition \u2192 esteem; challenging work \u2192 self-actualization. Motivation strategy must match the employee's unmet level."),
    P("Explain Herzberg's two-factor theory.", "Motivator factors (achievement, recognition, responsibility, growth) cause satisfaction; Hygiene factors (salary, working conditions, job security, company policy) prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate.", "Satisfiers vs dissatisfiers", "Improving hygiene only removes dissatisfaction; real motivation needs motivators. One person's hygiene factor can be another's motivator, so application is individual."),
    P("Explain Theory X and Theory Y (McGregor).", "Theory X: workers are lazy, dislike work, must be coerced/controlled and prefer direction \u2192 autocratic management. Theory Y: work is natural, people are self-directed and creative when committed \u2192 participative management.", "Two assumptions about workers", "Theory X managers supervise closely and punish; Theory Y managers delegate and empower. McGregor argued that assumptions drive management style, which becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy."),
    P("Compare line, line-and-staff, and functional organizations.", "Line: direct authority flows top-down (simple, clear, but overloads boss). Functional (Taylor): specialists supervise their own function (multiple bosses confuse). Line-and-staff: line keeps command, staff advise/support (advisors, engineers) - most common.", "Command flow and specialist advice", "In line-and-staff, staff have no direct authority over the line except within their own department \u2014 they help with expertise (HR, maintenance planning). Matrix organization adds project managers crossing functional heads."),
    P("What is span of control?", "Span of control is the number of subordinates a manager can effectively supervise. Narrow span \u2192 more levels (tall organization); wide span \u2192 fewer levels (flat organization).", "How many reports per supervisor", "Wide span suits simple standardized jobs; narrow span suits complex/stressful work. V. A. Graicunas's formula: interactions = n(2^n/2 + n \u2212 1)."),
    P("What is the difference between authority and responsibility?", "Authority is the right to command and make decisions; responsibility is the obligation to perform assigned tasks. Authority flows downward, accountability flows upward.", "Right to act vs duty to perform", "Fayol: authority and responsibility should be commensurate. Delegation transfers authority but not ultimate accountability."),
    P("What is time study? How is standard time computed?", "Time study (stopwatch method) determines the time a qualified worker takes for a task at normal pace. Standard Time = Normal Time + Allowances; Normal Time = Observed Time \u00D7 Rating Factor.", "Measure & standardize task time", "ST = NT \u00D7 (1 + allowance%). Allowances include personal (4\u20137%), fatigue, and delay allowances. Standard time forms the basis for piece rates, production planning and capacity."),
    P("What is motion study and what are therbligs?", "Motion study (Gilbreth) eliminates/minimizes wasteful movements, fixing the best sequence of motions. The 17 basic elements called therbligs (search, select, grasp, transport, hold, assemble, etc.) are the building blocks of every task.", "Simplify human motions", "Therbligs with visual control (like 'hold', 'search', 'wait') are eliminated or reduced. Principles of motion economy: both hands work, smooth continuous motions, tools pre-positioned, workplace arranged within normal reach."),
    P("What are the methods of work measurement?", "1. Time study (stopwatch). 2. Work sampling (ratio-delay). 3. Synthesis from standard data. 4. Analytical estimation. 5. Predetermined Motion Time Systems (PMTS, e.g., MTM/MOST). 6. Standard time data from past records.", "Ways to set time standards", "Work sampling observes random moments to find the percentage of time spent in categories (idle, working, delay). PMTS assigns time values to coded motions without a stopwatch."),
    P("Derive the Economic Order Quantity (EOQ).", "EOQ minimizes total inventory cost (ordering + carrying). EOQ = \u221A(2DS/H), where D = annual demand, S = order cost per order, H = holding cost per unit per year. Total cost = (D/Q)S + (Q/2)H.", "Square-root lot size formula", "At EOQ, ordering cost = carrying cost. Example D = 10000, S = 50, H = 1 \u2192 EOQ = \u221A(2\u00D710000\u00D750/1) = 1000 units. Assumptions: constant demand, no quantity discounts, instantaneous replenishment."),
    P("What is Reorder Point (ROP)?", "ROP = Lead-time demand + Safety stock = d\u00B7L + SS, where d = demand rate and L = lead time. When stock falls to ROP, place a new order.", "When to reorder", "For continuous review. EOQ decides HOW MUCH to order; ROP decides WHEN. Safety stock covers lead-time variability and prevents stock-out; ROP increases with demand variance."),
    P("Explain the ABC (Pareto) inventory classification.", "ABC classification ranks items by annual usage value: A items (70\u201380% value, ~10\u201320% of items) \u2192 tight control, frequent review, EOQ; B items medium; C items (many items, low value) \u2192 simple controls, bulk ordering.", "MANAGE BY VALUE - the 80/20 rule", "ABC analysis lets management focus expensive effort on A items while C items are kept by simple 'two-bin' systems. It is a permanent control based on annual demand \u00D7 unit cost."),
    P("What is a PERT network and what are its key elements?", "PERT (Program Evaluation and Review Technique) is a probabilistic network technique for uncertain activity times (3-estimate: optimistic a, most likely m, pessimistic b). Expected time te = (a + 4m + b)/6, variance \u03C3\u00B2 = ((b\u2212a)/6)\u00B2.", "3-estimate probabilistic scheduling", "Critical path = longest path through the network, controls project duration. PERT is for R&D/one-off projects where durations are uncertain; CPM is for routine, deterministic projects."),
    P("What is the critical path method (CPM)?", "CPM is a deterministic network technique that computes earliest start/finish (forward pass) and latest start/finish (backward pass), identifies the longest chain (critical path) where any delay lengthens the project, and enables cost-time trade-off (crashing).", "Deterministic longest-path scheduling", "Activities on the critical path have zero total float; delaying any one delays the project. Float of non-critical activities = LS\u2212ES = LF\u2212EF. Network must be a proper directed graph with dummy activities for dependencies."),
    P("Define total float, free float, and independent float.", "Total Float = LS \u2212 ES = LF \u2212 EF (delay possible without affecting project end). Free Float = ES(next activity) \u2212 EF(current) (without delaying successors). Independent Float = ES(succ) \u2212 LF(pred) \u2212 duration (can even start early within its own slack).", "Three slack measures", "Critical activities have TF = 0. Free float \u2264 Total float. Independent float protects both predecessor completion and successor start \u2014 least slack of all."),
    P("What is crashing in CPM?", "Crashing reduces project duration by assigning extra resources to critical activities, incurring added direct cost. Optimal duration is where total cost (direct + indirect) is minimum.", "Time-cost trade-off", "Crash only the critical path, and pick the activity with the lowest cost slope (cost slope = (crash cost \u2212 normal cost)/(normal time \u2212 crash time)). Recompute the critical path after each crash. Beyond a point, indirect cost savings exceed crash costs."),
    P("How does the Transportation Model work?", "The transportation problem ships goods from m sources to n destinations minimizing cost: standard method = find initial feasible solution (North-West Corner, Least Cost, VAM), then optimize (MODI/Stepping-stone) checking for degeneracy.", "Balancing supply and demand at minimum cost", "Vogel's Approximation Method (VAM) gives near-optimal start. Optimality test: compute opportunity cost for unoccupied cells; if all \u2265 0 the solution is optimal; else re-allocate. Degeneracy (fewer occupied cells than m + n \u2212 1) requires artificial entries."),
    P("Explain the Assignment (Hungarian) problem.", "The assignment problem pairs n jobs to n machines (one-to-one) minimizing total cost/time. Hungarian method: subtract row minima and column minima, cover all zeros with minimum lines; if lines = n, optimum; else row/column adjustment and repeat.", "One-to-one optimal matching", "Assignment is a 0\u20131 transportation problem with m = n and degenerate: exactly n occupied cells, no re-allocation allowed. Used for job\u2013machine, man\u2013task, area\u2013territory assignments."),
    P("How do you solve a linear programming problem graphically?", "LP: maximize/minimize Z = c\u00B7x subject to linear constraints. Graphical method: plot constraints, identify feasible region, evaluate Z at each corner (vertex) point; the optimum lies at a vertex (corner point theorem).", "Optimum at a corner of feasible region", "Only two-variable problems are solved graphically. Steps: plot lines, shade feasible region, find vertices, compute Z, pick best. If region is unbounded, Z may be unbounded (maximization)."),
    P("What is the simplex method?", "Simplex is an iterative algebraic method for linear programming with n variables: start at a basic feasible solution, check optimality by reduced cost, and pivot into the basis while improving the objective until no negative/positive reduced costs remain.", "Tabular iteration of LP corner solutions", "Surplus/slack variables convert inequalities into equalities. Degeneracy and cycling are handled by bespoke rules. It extends the corner-point idea to high dimensions; duality gives sensitivity analysis."),
    P("Write the basic relationships for M/M/1 queue.", "An M/M/1 queue has Poisson arrivals, exponential service, single server. Utilization \u03C1 = \u03BB/\u03BC. L = \u03BB/(\u03BC\u2212\u03BB), Lq = \u03BB\u00B2/(\u03BC(\u03BC\u2212\u03BB)), W = 1/(\u03BC\u2212\u03BB), Wq = \u03BB/(\u03BC(\u03BC\u2212\u03BB)). Stability needs \u03C1 < 1.", "Single-server queue equations", "Example \u03BB = 4/h, \u03BC = 5/h: \u03C1 = 0.8; W = 1/(5\u22124) = 1 h wait total; L = 4/(5\u22124) = 4 customers. Reduces operators' queue, guides staffing decisions."),
    P("What is break-even analysis?", "Break-even point (BEP) is where revenue equals total cost: BEP (units) = Fixed Cost / (Selling price \u2212 Variable cost per unit). Contribution = SP \u2212 VC.", "Where profit starts", "Example FC = 100000, SP = 100, VC = 60 \u2192 BEP = 100000/40 = 2500 units. Above BEP, every unit contributes \u20B940 profit. Margin of safety = (Sales \u2212 BEP sales); lower BEP (higher contribution) is better."),
    P("List and explain the main types of plant layout.", "1. Process/Functional (machines grouped by type) \u2014 job shops, flexible. 2. Product/Line (machines in sequence) \u2014 mass production. 3. Group/GT (cells for part families). 4. Fixed-position (large objects, product stays) \u2014 ships, turbines. 5. Combination.", "How machines are arranged", "Product layout has low handling cost and high output but is rigid; process layout is flexible but has high handling. Cellular (GT) layout gives line advantages with batch flexibility."),
    P("What is statistical quality control (SQC)?", "SQC uses statistical methods to monitor and control quality: control charts (X\u0304, R for variables; p, c for attributes), acceptance sampling plans, and process capability studies to detect assignable causes vs common-cause variation.", "Charts + sampling to control quality", "X\u0304 & R charts track process mean and spread; control limits usually \u00B13\u03C3. Points outside limits = assignable cause present \u2192 investigate. p-chart tracks fraction defective; c-chart tracks number of defects per unit."),
    P("What is the difference between control charts and acceptance sampling?", "Control charts monitor the PROCESS continuously during production (preventive, indicates when to adjust). Acceptance sampling inspects a SAMPLE of finished LOTS to decide accept/reject (appraisal of output after production).", "Process control vs lot acceptance", "Control charts act during manufacture; sampling decides whether to accept a delivered batch. AQL (acceptable quality level) and consumer's risk are the concepts behind sampling plans."),
    P("What is PPC (Production Planning & Control)?", "PPC coordinates planning (routing, scheduling, loading, estimating) and control (dispatching, expediting, follow-up) to produce right quantity, quality, at the right time and cost.", "Planning + control of production", "Functions: Routing (sequence), Scheduling (timing), Loading (capacity assignment), Dispatching (release orders), Expediting (hastening), Inspection (verify). Tools: Gantt charts, load charts, MRP, ERP."),
    P("What is a Gantt chart?", "A Gantt chart is a bar chart showing each job/activity's start and end time against the calendar; the earliest simple scheduling tool for production and projects.", "Horizontal bar schedule", "Gantt charts are clear to read but do not show activity dependencies. For dependent project work, PERT/CPM is used; Gantt remains a powerful display (Excel/MS Project use it)."),
    P("What is kaizen?", "Kaizen (Japanese, 'improvement') is continuous incremental improvement of processes, involving all employees in small daily improvements \u2014 the core of lean manufacturing.", "Every day, small improvements", "Kaizen events (workshops) focus on eliminating waste (muda): overproduction, waiting, transport, over-processing, inventory, motion, defects. Contrast with innovation/breakthrough improvement (kaikaku)."),
    P("List the 5S's.", "Sort (Seiri), Set-in-order (Seiton), Shine (Seiso), Standardize (Seiketsu), Sustain (Shitsuke).", "Lean housekeeping foundation", "5S creates a visual, disciplined workplace, reduces search time and accidents, and is the foundation for TPM, kaizen and quality programs."),
    P("What is quality cost (COQ)?", "Quality costs: Prevention (training, planning), Appraisal (inspection, testing), Internal Failure (scrap, rework), External Failure (warranty, returns, brand loss). The optimum is where prevention/appraisal just offset failure costs. Also 'quality is free' (Crosby).", "Four cost categories of quality", "External failure is the most expensive because a customer is affected. Investments in prevention reduce total COQ and improve competitiveness (Juran, Deming, Crosby schools). Deming's 14 points stress 'quality by design', not inspection alone.")
  ];

  SSC_JE_MECHENG.esepre = [
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "Which of the following is NOT a classification of 'preventive maintenance'?", "A. Planned maintenance  B. Predictive maintenance  C. Run-to-failure  D. Break-in maintenance", "Think which one is passive", "Run-to-failure is not preventive \u2014 it is a deliberate policy of no action until breakdown. Preventive maintenance includes time-based (planned) and condition-based (predictive) tasks. Answer: C."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "The Kelvin\u2013Planck statement is about:", "A. Conversion of heat fully into work  B. Spontaneous flow of heat from cold to hot  C. Perpetual motion machine of the first kind  D. Both A and C", "Relates to impossible engines", "Kelvin\u2013Planck: it is impossible to construct a heat engine operating in a cycle that converts ALL heat absorbed into work, with no heat rejection. This directly forbids a PMM of the first kind. Answer: D."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "For the same compression ratio, the thermal efficiency order is:", "A. Otto > Diesel > Dual  B. Diesel > Otto > Dual  C. Otto > Dual > Diesel  D. Dual > Diesel > Otto", "Otto burns at constant volume", "At fixed compression ratio, constant-volume (Otto) gives the highest ideal efficiency, then Dual (mixed), then constant-pressure (Diesel). At same peak pressure, Diesel > Dual > Otto. Answer: C."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "A fluid with Prandtl number far greater than 1 flows over a flat plate. Then:", "A. Thermal boundary layer is thicker than velocity boundary layer  B. Velocity boundary layer is thicker  C. Both are equal  D. Nusselt number depends on Grashof number", "\u03B4v/\u03B4t \u221D Pr^{1/3}", "Velocity boundary layer thickness \u03B4v = \u03B4t\u00B7Pr^{1/3}. For Pr >> 1 (oils, glycerine), the velocity layer is thicker than the thermal layer. Answer: B."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "In a convergent\u2013divergent nozzle, the maximum velocity is obtained at the:", "A. Convergent entrance  B. Throat  C. Divergent exit  D. Anywhere in the divergent part", "Choked at throat = Mach 1", "The throat of a C-D nozzle is where flow reaches sonic velocity (Mach 1). With a proper pressure ratio, it accelerates to supersonic in the divergent part \u2014 maximum velocity at the exit. Answer: C."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "The Poisson's ratio of an incompressible elastic material is:", "A. 0  B. 0.5  C. 0.25  D. \u22121", "Bulks modulus \u2192 infinity", "For incompressible material, bulk modulus K \u2192 \u221E, and K = E/[3(1\u22122\u00B5)]. Finite E then requires 1\u22122\u00B5 = 0, so \u00B5 = 0.5 (rubber). Steel \u00B5 \u2248 0.3. Answer: B."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "In a shaft under torsion, the shear stress varies:", "A. Uniformly across section  B. Parabolically  C. Linearly from centre to surface  D. Inversely as radius", "Torsion formula \u03C4/r = T/J", "In pure torsion \u03C4 = T\u00B7r/J, stress is zero at the centre and maximum at the outer surface \u2014 varies linearly with radius. Answer: C."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "Which of the following is a secondary quantity in the Kelvin\u2013Planck or rather in MPI measurement?", "A. Depth of cut  B. Cutting force  C. Cutting temperature  D. Tool wear", "Measurement in machining", "In machining metrology, force, temperature and wear are 'secondary' (derived) quantities measured for research; depth of cut is a direct/primary setting. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "For stable equilibrium of a floating body, the metacentre must be:", "A. Above the centre of gravity  B. Below the centre of gravity  C. At the centre of gravity  D. At the centre of buoyancy", "Rocking balance condition", "Stability: GM > 0 (metacentre above CG). If M lies below G, the body is unstable and capsizes. GM = distance between metacentre and centre of gravity. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "The degree of reaction of an impulse turbine is:", "A. 0  B. 0.5  C. 1  D. \u22121", "Impulse = no pressure drop over rotor", "R = enthalpy drop in rotor / total enthalpy drop. In an impulse turbine all expansion happens in nozzles, so rotor drop = 0 and R = 0. For a 50% reaction turbine, R = 0.5 (Parson). Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "NPSH of a centrifugal pump is provided to avoid:", "A. Hydraulic jump  B. Cavitation  C. Surging  D. Water hammer", "Minimum inlet head requirement", "Net Positive Suction Head ensures the local static head at the impeller eye stays above vapor pressure so bubbles do not form and collapse (cavitation). Answer: B."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "A flywheel is used to control:", "A. Variation in speed over a cycle  B. Speed fluctuation over many cycles  C. Governor isochronism  D. Mean speed", "Cyclic fluctuation", "A flywheel stores/releases energy to smooth the cyclic variation of speed within one revolution. A governor controls mean speed over long periods (speed regulation). Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "In LMTD for heat exchangers, counter flow gives:", "A. Higher mean temperature difference than parallel flow  B. Lower than parallel flow  C. Same as parallel flow  D. Zero in the limit", "Hot and cold always approach", "In counter flow the temperature difference stays more uniform and the outlet can exceed hot-outlet limits, so LMTD is higher and a smaller area is needed for the same duty. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "Superheating steam in the Rankine cycle mainly:", "A. Increases cycle efficiency  B. Decreases dryness fraction in the turbine  C. Reduces pump work  D. Increases boiler pressure", "Raises T_m; cuts last-stage wetness", "Superheat raises the mean temperature of heat addition (higher \u03B7) and reduces moisture in the low-pressure turbine stages (better blade life). It does not directly change pump work. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "In a gear pair, 'module' is defined as:", "A. Number of teeth per unit pitch-circle diameter  B. Pitch circle diameter per tooth  C. Ratio of circular pitch to \u03C0  D. Addendum/pitch-circle ratio", "Module m = d/z = p/\u03C0", "Module m = pitch circle diameter / number of teeth = circular pitch / \u03C0, in mm. It is the internationally standardized size parameter; diametral pitch = inverse in inches. Answer: B (m = D/T)."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "For a beam with triangular load peaking at the fixed end of a cantilever, the bending moment diagram is:", "A. Linear  B. Parabolic  C. Cubic  D. Constant", "Load is linear \u2192 integrate twice", "Shear force = area of load diagram (quadratic); bending moment = integral of shear (cubic). For linear load the SFD is a parabola and BMD a cubic. Answer: C."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "In a journal bearing, the load carrying capacity arises mainly because of:", "A. Hydrodynamic pressure wedge  B. Hydrostatic pump  C. Boundary film  D. Rolling elements", "Wedge film build-up", "Relative motion draws lubricant into a converging wedge, generating hydrodynamic pressure that separates the surfaces \u2014 the basis of the Reynolds equation. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "The cam that provides the least jerk is:", "A. Cycloidal  B. Simple harmonic  C. Modified trapezoidal  D. Parabolic", "Acceleration continuity", "Cycloidal cam acceleration is a smooth sine wave, eliminating jerk spikes at the start/end, giving the smoothest follower motion and least noise. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "Bourdon gauge measures:", "A. Absolute pressure  B. Gauge pressure  C. Vacuum  D. Pressure difference only", "Zero-relative reading", "The Bourdon tube deflects relative to atmospheric pressure, so it reads gauge pressure (pressure above or below atmosphere). For absolute pressure, a barometer/special cells are used. Answer: B."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "Dry bulb temperature of air in an evaporative cooler:", "A. Decreases  B. Increases  C. Remains constant  D. Reaches dew point", "Sensible cooling by evaporation", "Evaporative cooling is essentially adiabatic saturation: DBT falls while WBT stays nearly constant, humidity increases. Evaporation requires air not already saturated. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "The coefficient of performance of a reversed Carnot refrigerator working between T2 (cold) and T1 (hot):", "A. T2/(T1\u2212T2)  B. T1/(T1\u2212T2)  C. T1/T2  D. (T1\u2212T2)/T1", "Refrigerator COP formula", "COP (refrigerator) = Q2/W = T2/(T1\u2212T2); COP (heat pump) = T1/(T1\u2212T2). Reversed Carnot sets the upper limit for all refrigerators. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "In flow measurement, the venturimeter gives the most accurate reading but the orificemeter is cheaper. Which is TRUE?", "A. Cd venturi > Cd orifice  B. Cd orifice > Cd venturi  C. Both equal  D. Neither depends on Reynolds number", "Discharge coefficient comparison", "Cd of a venturimeter (\u2248 0.95\u20130.99) is higher than an orificemeter (\u2248 0.62) because the venturi has smooth convergent\u2013divergent walls with little loss. Answer: A."),
    E("ESE Prelims (GE)", "In the Si system, 1 bar is approximately equal to:", "A. 1 kgf/cm\u00B2  B. 760 mm of Hg  C. 1 atm  D. 14.7 kPa", "Pressure unit conversion", "1 bar = 10^5 Pa = 100 kPa. 1 standard atm = 1.013 bar = 760 mm Hg = 14.7 psi. 1 kgf/cm\u00B2 \u2248 0.981 bar. Answer: B (760 mm Hg = 1 atm \u2248 1.013 bar; closest is B \u2014 760 mmHg)."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "Pick the odd one out (all relate to a rolling bearing):", "A. Inner race  B. Cage  C. Land  D. Rolling element", "Identify what does not belong", "Land is the machined edge of a plain journal/thrust bearing adjacent to the groove; races, cage and rolling elements belong to ball/roller bearings. Answer: C."),
    E("ESE Prelims (ME)", "For successful dynamic balancing of a rotating disc, the minimum number of balance masses needed is:", "A. One  B. Two  C. Three  D. Four", "Single-plane correction", "A thin rotor needs single-plane balancing; one imbalance correction weight can cancel a single unbalance force vector (magnitude + angle = one mass). Two planes are needed for long rotors (dynamic/couple). Answer: A as a minimum.")
  ];

  SSC_JE_MECHENG.esemain = [
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "With a neat sketch explain the working of a 4-stroke spark-ignition engine and its PV diagram.", "Four strokes: Suction, Compression, Power, Exhaust. Air-fuel mixture enters during suction; mixture is compressed (1\u21922); spark ignites, burning and expanding (2\u21923 power); exhaust valve opens and gases leave (4\u21921).", "Mention the valve & piston events per stroke", "PV sketch:\n\n<pre>P |           3-----2       |          /       |         /        |      /1         |  4---/           |__/_______________ V</pre>\nWhere 1\u21922 = compression, 2\u21923 = combustion (heat add at ~constant volume), 3\u21924 = expansion/power, 4\u21921 = exhaust (heat reject at ~constant volume). Two revolutions per cycle (720\u00B0). Spark advances before TDC; exhaust valve opens slightly before BDC to drop pressure."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "What is a hydraulic jump? Derive the relation between conjugate depths for a rectangular channel.", "A hydraulic jump is the abrupt transition of supercritical flow to subcritical flow with a steep rise in surface, intense turbulence, energy dissipation and air entrainment.", "Use momentum (specific force) equation", "For a rectangular channel the specific force (momentum) is constant across the jump:\n\nF = Q\u00B2/(gA) + Z\u0304A = constant.\n\nFor unit width, Q = V1\u00B7y1 = V2\u00B7y2, and A = y\u00B71:\n\ny2/y1 = [\u221A(1 + 8\u00B7Fr1\u00B2) \u2212 1]/2.\n\nHere Fr1 = V1/\u221A(g\u00B7y1) is the upstream Froude number (>1). The jump keeps F (specific momentum) constant, NOT energy \u2014 energy is dissipated in turbulence."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "For a simply supported beam carrying a central point load, draw the SFD and BMD and derive the maximum bending moment.", "A simply supported beam, span L, load W at centre: reactions RA = RB = W/2. SFD: +W/2 on the left half, \u2212W/2 on the right. BMD: triangular, zero at supports, peak WL/4 at centre.", "Cut the beam and sum forces/moments", "Taking a cut at distance x left of centre: V = W/2, M = (W/2)x. At x = L/2, Mmax = WL/4.\n\n<pre>      W\n      |\n  ____v____\nRA |  /\   | RB    SFD: __|__/\\__.__/|_\n   |_/____\\_|\n       WL/4\n        /\\    BMD:   /\\\n       /  \\         /  \\        0 at ends, peak WL/4 in the middle</pre>\n\nThe SFD is unit step-wise constant at \u00B1W/2, the BMD linear V-shaped with peak WL/4. Sign convention: sagging positive below the axis."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Explain the isentropic (adiabatic-reversible) relations for a perfect gas and their practical use.", "PV^\u03B3 = constant, TV^(\u03B3\u22121) = constant, T/P^((\u03B3\u22121)/\u03B3) = constant; work of an isentropic process = (\u03A3P1V1 \u2212 P2V2)/(\u03B3\u22121).", "Three forms of PV^gamma = C", "These follow from the First Law with ds = 0 and perfect-gas state equations, assuming constant specific heats and a reversible adiabatic path. They model compression/expansion in compressors, turbines, nozzles, and engines, enabling pressure\u2013temperature\u2013volume interconversion when no heat transfer occurs."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Describe the working of a centrifugal pump with a velocity triangle and give the Euler head equation.", "A centrifugal pump raises liquid using centrifugal force: liquid enters axially at the impeller eye, is flung radially outward by rotating vanes and collected in a volute casing which converts kinetic energy into pressure.", "Draw inlet & outlet velocity triangles", "Euler head H_E = (u2\u00B7Vw2 \u2212 u1\u00B7Vw1)/g. For no inlet whirl in a normal radial entry, Vw1 = 0, so H_E = u2\u00B7Vw2/g.\n\n<pre>          \u03B2 (vane angle)\n   Tangent u2 \u2192__/\n             \\\n   V (abs)  \\  Vr (relative)\n    /\n   Vw2 (whirl)  \u2192</pre>\n\nVolute casing converts high velocity into head; diffuser vanes in multistage pumps do the same. Theoretical head depends on vane outlet angle \u03B2 (backward-curved vanes give stable, efficient performance)."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "With a T-s diagram explain the vapour compression refrigeration cycle and its ideal COP.", "The cycle: 1\u21922 isentropic compression, 2\u21923 constant-pressure condensation, 3\u21924 throttling in an expansion valve (h = constant), 4\u21921 constant-pressure evaporation absorbing heat Qe.", "Trace 4 processes on T-s", "Sketch (T-s):\n\n<pre>T\n| 2----- saturated? (2\u21923 condenser)\n|___3     \n|    \\   \n|     \\__1\u21924 evaporator (4\u21921 horizontal-ish)\n|______|________ s</pre>\n\nRefrigerating effect Qe = h1 \u2212 h4; work W = h2 \u2212 h1; COP = (h1\u2212h4)/(h2\u2212h1). Real effects: pressure drops in piping, non-isentropic compression, subcooling improves COP, superheating at suction protects compressor and raises discharge temperature. Refrigerant R-134a replaced R-12; ammonia and CO2 (transcritical) are also used."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Derive the bending stress formula \u03C3 = My/I and mention its assumptions.", "For a beam in pure bending: plane sections remain plane, and longitudinal strain varies linearly from the neutral axis \u2014 \u03B5 = y/\u03C1 (y measured from the neutral axis). Hooke's law gives \u03C3 = E\u00B7y/\u03C1. Balancing moment gives M = (E/\u03C1)I, hence \u03C3 = My/I.", "Strain linear in y \u2192 stress linear; M = EI/\u03C1", "Assumptions (Euler\u2013Bernoulli): material is homogeneous, isotropic, elastic & follows Hooke's law, stresses within proportional limit; plane transverse sections remain plane and normal; no shear-force bending (pure bending) effect; loads act in a plane of symmetry. Maximum bending stress occurs at the extreme fibre, y = c: \u03C3max = Mc/I."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Compare Otto, Diesel and Dual cycles on the same P-V diagram; which gives the highest efficiency for the same compression ratio?", "Sketch (P-V): Otto has combustion at constant volume (vertical), Diesel at constant pressure (horizontal), Dual lies in between (constant volume + constant pressure). For the same compression ratio Otto > Dual > Diesel; for the same peak pressure Diesel > Dual > Otto.", "Trace the three cycles on one diagram", "<pre>P|   / Otto curve\n |  / 2_3(Const V)\n |  |  3'---''' 3\"(Const P)\n |  |     |  \\\n |  |    _/__4   \\_______\n | 1-----         |   \n |__________________ V>  </pre>\n\nReason: Otto adds heat at the highest average temperature interval. In Dual, heat add is split, so mean heat-addition temperature is between Otto and Diesel, placing its efficiency between them too."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Explain the working of a Pelton turbine and sketch its velocity triangles.", "Pelton is an impulse turbine: a high-pressure jet from a nozzle strikes splitter buckets on the runner rim; all pressure drop occurs in the nozzle, none across the buckets; the jet exits both sides of the bucket.", "Impulse turbine on an impulse wheel", "Velocity triangle (inlet):\n\n<pre>        jet velocity V1 \u2192  (blade speed u = \u03C9R)\n           |\\ boundary\\\n           |  \\   \n  u \u2192          r1 = V1 \u2212 u</pre>\n\nMaximum hydraulic efficiency occurs when bucket speed u \u2248 V1/2. Theoretical power P = \u03C1Q(V1 \u2212 u)(1 + k cos\u03C6)u; efficiency is best at u/V1 = (1 + k cos\u03C6)/2, with k = bucket smoothness and \u03C6 \u2248 165\u00B0 deflection. Used for high head (100\u20131800 m) and low discharge."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Explain Euler's buckling load for columns, and when to use Euler vs Rankine formula.", "Euler critical load Pe = \u03C0\u00B2EI/(Le\u00B2), where Le = effective length (depends on end conditions: fixed\u2013fixed 0.5L, one-fixed one-pinned 0.7L, pinned\u2013pinned L, fixed\u2013free 2L). Valid only in the elastic range (slenderness ratio large, \u03BB > \u03BBc).", "Pe = PI\u00B2EI/Le\u00B2, small columns fail by crushing", "For short/stocky columns, failure is by crushing stress, not buckling \u2014 use Rankine's parabolic formula:\n\n1/Pr = 1/Pc + 1/Pe.\n\nRankine load Pr = \u03C3c\u00B7A / [1 + a\u00B7(\u03BB)\u00B2], where \u03BB = Le/k (k = least radius of gyration) and a = \u03C3c/(\u03C0\u00B2E). Euler suits long slender struts; Rankine applies over the full slenderness range from stubby to slender columns."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Explain why counter-flow heat exchangers are preferred over parallel flow, with a temperature diagram.", "In counter flow, the two streams move in opposite directions, so one outlet can approach the other inlet temperature, giving a larger and more uniform temperature difference, hence a smaller heat-transfer area for the same duty.", "Draw T vs length for both; note X-over", "Sketch (T vs length):\n\n<pre>Parallel:             Counter:   \nTh1 |\\__          Th1 |\\________\n    |   \\               |         \\ Tc2\n    |    Tc2_          ....... \nTc1 |_______             |____ Tc1\n    \\_______________  \n</pre>\n\nCounter flow also gives 'temperature cross', letting the cold outlet exceed the parallel-flow hot outlet limit. Result: \u0394Tlm,counter > \u0394Tlm,parallel \u2192 smaller required area and higher effectiveness. Used in condensers, evaporators, economizers and regenerators."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "A helical compression spring of wire diameter d, coil diameter D and n active coils carries load W. Derive the deflection and the maximum shear stress (including curvature effect).", "Torsion + direct shear: \u03C4max = (8WD/\u03C0d\u00B3) \u00D7 Ks; deflection \u03B4 = 8WD\u00B3n/(Gd\u2074).", "Wahl factor K = (4C\u22121)/(4C\u22124) + 0.615/C", "The spring index C = D/d. Stresses combine torsion (8WD/\u03C0d\u00B3) and direct shear; Wahl factor K_w accounts for curvature and direct shear. So\n\n\u03C4max = K_w(8WD)/(\u03C0d\u00B3),   \u03B4 = 8WD\u00B3n/(Gd\u2074).\n\nStiffness k = W/\u03B4 = Gd\u2074/(8D\u00B3n). Surge is prevented by proper end conditions and by avoiding resonance with operating frequency."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "What are governors? Explain isochronism and hunting.", "A governor regulates mean speed automatically by varying the energy supply, balancing the engine against load changes using centrifugal feedback (e.g., Watt, Porter, Hartnell governors).", "Speed control over cycles, not within a cycle", "Isochronism: a governor is isochronous if for every load the same speed is maintained \u2014 zero speed drop from no-load to full-load (ideal, practically achieved by increasing spring/weight sensitivity). Hunting: violent cyclic oscillation of the governor about the mean position, caused by excessive sensitivity; it is a continuous under/over-correction cycle and must be avoided by proper damping."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Explain the working of a latent-heat (sensible heat factor based) psychrometric process: sensible heating of air with DBT and WBT changes.", "Sensible heating adds heat without adding moisture: DBT increases, WBT increases slightly, specific humidity stays constant, RH falls, and the process is a horizontal line to the right on the psychrometric chart.", "Horizontal rightward line at constant w", "Sensible Heat Factor SHF = Qs/(Qs + Ql). For pure sensible heating SHF = 1.0. WBT increase is small because latent contribution is unchanged. Applications: air preheaters, reheat coils, fan-heat pick-up. The process requires the surface temperature > air DBT."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Define reliability; how is redundancy used, and what is a 2-out-of-3 (2oo3) system?", "Reliability R(t) = P(no failure in [0,t]) = e^{\u2212\u03BBt} for constant \u03BB. Redundancy adds parallel paths. A 2oo3 voting system is functional if at least 2 of its 3 identical channels work.", "Series multiplies, parallel combines", "For a 2oo3 system with channel reliability R:\n\nR_2oo3 = 3R\u00B2(1\u2212R) + R\u00B3 = 3R\u00B2 \u2212 2R\u00B3.\n\nIf R = 0.9: R_2oo3 = 3(0.81)(0.1) + 0.729 = 0.972, better than a single channel (0.9) yet it still tolerates one sensor disagreement \u2014 used in safety instrumented systems (fire/gas protection) and voting logic controllers."),
    E("ESE Mains (ME)", "Draw a block diagram of a closed-loop servo speed control using a tachometer and explain the role of each block.", "Blocks: Reference speed (set point) \u2192 Comparator (error) \u2192 Controller (PI/PID) \u2192 Power amplifier \u2192 DC motor; tachometer (feedback) \u2192 comparator completes the loop.", "Feedback loop components & signals", "<pre>\nRef \u2192 (+)e\u2192 [Controller] \u2192 [Amplifier] \u2192 [Motor+Load] \u2192 \u03C9\n              \u2191                          |\n              \u2190 [Tachometer] <\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014\u2014- |</pre>\n\nThe tachometer senses actual speed and returns a voltage; the comparator finds the error; the controller shapes the correction (reduces steady error, adds damping); the power amplifier drives the motor. This negative feedback makes output robust to load/torque disturbances and supply variation.")
  ];

  if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
    window.SSC_JE_MECHENG = SSC_JE_MECHENG;
  }
  if (typeof module !== 'undefined') {
    module.exports = SSC_JE_MECHENG;
  }
})();